==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|frm|desplacer}} (French: {{m|fr|déplacer}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɪsˈpleɪs/|/dɪzˈpleɪs/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-displace.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/dɪsˈpleɪs/|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪs|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To put out of place; to disarrange.
# To [[move]] something, or someone, especially to [[forcibly]] move [[people]] from their [[homeland]].
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-india-65522719|date=2023-05-08|title=Manipur: Thousands displaced as ethnic clashes grip north-eastern state|passage=Manipur: Thousands '''displaced''' as ethnic clashes grip north-eastern state|work=BBC News}}
# To [[supplant]], or take the place of something or someone; to [[substitute]].
# To [[replace]], on account of being [[superior]] to or more [[suitable]] than that which is being replaced.
#: {{ux|en|Electronic calculators soon '''displaced''' the older mechanical kind.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|month=January|author=Cecil J. Allen|title=British Locomotive Practice and Performance|journal=Railway Magazine|page=13|text=All have gone the same way, and since the war have '''displaced''' up-to-date steam power on all their principal services by the all-conquering diesel.}}
# {{lb|en|of a floating ship}} To have a [[weight]] equal to that of the [[water]] displaced.
# {{lb|en|psychology}} To [[repress]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2017|author=Megan Garber|title=The Case for Shyness|journal=The Atlantic|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/entertainment/archive/2017/02/the-case-for-shyness/516933/|text=Freud considered shyness to be evidence of '''displaced''' narcissism.}}

